{"name":"Campfire Smoke","hex":"#d5d1cb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"campfire","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fire at a campground or on a camping trip, often used for cooking, to provide light and heat, to drive away bugs, and as a focal point for sitting around in the evening and talking, telling stories, and singing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/campfire"},{"term":"smoke","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The visible vapor/vapour, gases, and fine particles given off by burning or smoldering material.","A cigarette."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To inhale and exhale the smoke from a burning cigarette or cigar, burning material in a pipe, etc.","To inhale and exhale tobacco smoke."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/smoke"}],"entries":[{"title":"Campfire","blocks":[{"text":"A campfire is a fire at a campsite that provides light, warmth, and heat for cooking. It can also serve as a beacon and an insect and predator deterrent. Established campgrounds often provide a stone or steel fire ring for safety. Campfires are a popular feature of camping. At summer camps, the word campfire often refers to an event at which there is a fire. Some camps refer to the fire itself as a campfire."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Campfire_Pinecone.png/330px-Campfire_Pinecone.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Campfire","wikidata":"Q329981"},{"title":"Smoke","blocks":[{"text":"Smoke is an aerosol emitted when a material undergoes combustion or pyrolysis, together with the quantity of air that is entrained or otherwise mixed into the mass. It is commonly an unwanted by-product of fires, but may also be used for pest control (fumigation), communication, defensive and offensive capabilities in the military, cooking, or smoking. It is used in rituals where incense, sage, or resin is burned to produce a smell for spiritual or magical purposes. It can also be a flavoring agent and preservative."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Ognisko_ubt_0126.jpeg/330px-Ognisko_ubt_0126.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smoke","wikidata":"Q130768"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Montana_16_bg_062406.jpg/500px-Montana_16_bg_062406.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Montana_16_bg_062406.jpg/500px-Montana_16_bg_062406.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A small fire in a backyard fire pit. Dousing a campfire or fireplace with soapy water and detergent is not a good idea. While it may seem like a good way to extinguish the fire, the soapy water can actually cause things to become worse. Unless the fire has been extinguished and only hot ash remains.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMontana_16_bg_062406.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/1/18/Pitchwood.jpg/500px-Pitchwood.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/1/18/Pitchwood.jpg/1280px-Pitchwood.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/1/18/Pitchwood.jpg/500px-Pitchwood.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pitchwood from a fir stump","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APitchwood.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Teepee_Camp_Fire_in_the_Pacific_Northwest.jpg/500px-Teepee_Camp_Fire_in_the_Pacific_Northwest.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimed